                <section class="how-to-use-section content-section">
                    <h2 class="section-title">How to Use Our Calorie & Macro Calculator</h2>
                    <ol>
                        <li><strong>Enter your personal details:</strong> Provide your gender, age, height, and weight. Select your preferred unit system (Imperial or Metric).</li>
                        <li><strong>Select your activity level:</strong> Choose the activity level that best describes your daily physical routine from the dropdown menu.</li>
                        <li><strong>Choose your preferred BMR formula:</strong> Select either the Mifflin-St Jeor or Harris-Benedict formula for Basal Metabolic Rate calculation. Mifflin-St Jeor is generally recommended.</li>
                        <li><strong>Specify your primary goal:</strong> Indicate your primary objective: weight loss, weight maintenance, or muscle gain.</li>
                        <li><strong>Click "Calculate"</strong> to reveal your personalized daily calorie and macronutrient recommendations.</li>
                    </ol>
                </section>

                <section class="content-section"> 
                    <h2 class="section-title">Understanding Your Results: BMR, TDEE, and Macronutrients Explained</h2>
                    <h3>What is Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR)?</h3>
                    <p>BMR is the minimum number of calories your body needs to perform basic, life-sustaining functions at rest, such as breathing, circulation, and cell production. It represents the energy expended while in a state of complete rest.</p>
                    <h3>What is Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE)?</h3>
                    <p>TDEE is the total number of calories your body burns in a 24-hour period, including your BMR and the energy expended through physical activity, digestion, and other daily movements. It's the most accurate representation of your daily calorie needs.</p>
                    <h3>The Role of Macronutrients (Macros)</h3>
                    <p>Macronutrients are the nutrients your body needs in large quantities to provide energy and support various bodily functions. They include Protein (4 cal/g), Carbohydrates (4 cal/g), and Fat (9 cal/g).</p>
                </section>

                <section class="content-section">
                    <h2 class="section-title">Choosing Your Formula: Mifflin-St Jeor vs. Harris-Benedict</h2>
                    <h3>Mifflin-St Jeor Equation:</h3>
                    <p>The Mifflin-St Jeor equation is generally considered more accurate for today's average adult, as it was derived from a larger, more diverse dataset. It is commonly preferred due to its contemporary research basis.</p>
                    <p><strong>Men:</strong> $$(10 \times \text{weight\_kg}) + (6.25 \times \text{height\_cm}) - (5 \times \text{age}) + 5$$</p>
                    <p><strong>Women:</strong> $$(10 \times \text{weight\_kg}) + (6.25 \times \text{height\_cm}) - (5 \times \text{age}) - 161$$</p>
                    <h3>Revised Harris-Benedict Equation:</h3>
                    <p>The Harris-Benedict equation (revised in 1984) was developed earlier and tends to overestimate BMR in some modern populations. It provides a historical context for BMR calculation methods.</p>
                    <p><strong>Men:</strong> $$88.362 + (13.397 \times \text{weight\_kg}) + (4.799 \times \text{height\_cm}) - (5.677 \times \text{age})$$</p>
                    <p><strong>Women:</strong> $$447.593 + (9.247 \times \text{weight\_kg}) + (3.098 \times \text{height\_cm}) - (4.330 \times \text{age})$$</p>
                    <table class="data-table">
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Formula</th>
                                <th>Strengths</th>
                                <th>Weaknesses</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Mifflin-St Jeor</td>
                                <td>More modern, generally more accurate for average adults.</td>
                                <td>May still have minor inaccuracies for extreme body compositions.</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Revised Harris-Benedict</td>
                                <td>Historically significant, widely recognized.</td>
                                <td>Tends to overestimate BMR in some modern populations.</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </section>

                <section class="content-section">
                    <h2 class="section-title">Customizing Macros for Your Goals</h2>
                    <h3>Macros for Weight Loss:</h3>
                    <p>To lose weight, you generally need to consume fewer calories than your TDEE, creating a calorie deficit. High protein intake is crucial for muscle retention during weight loss. Suggested macro percentages: 35% Protein, 40% Carbohydrates, 25% Fat.</p>
                    <h3>Macros for Weight Maintenance:</h3>
                    <p>For weight maintenance, you should aim to consume calories roughly equal to your TDEE. A balanced macronutrient intake supports overall health and energy levels. Suggested macro percentages: 25% Protein, 50% Carbohydrates, 25% Fat.</p>
                    <h3>Macros for Muscle Gain (Bulking):</h3>
                    <p>Muscle gain typically requires a calorie surplus (more than your TDEE). Adequate protein and carbohydrates are essential for muscle protein synthesis and recovery. Suggested macro percentages: 30% Protein, 45% Carbohydrates, 25% Fat.</p>
                    <table class="data-table">
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Goal</th>
                                <th>Protein (%)</th>
                                <th>Carbohydrates (%)</th>
                                <th>Fat (%)</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Weight Loss</td>
                                <td>35%</td>
                                <td>40%</td>
                                <td>25%</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Maintenance</td>
                                <td>25%</td>
                                <td>50%</td>
                                <td>25%</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Muscle Gain</td>
                                <td>30%</td>
                                <td>45%</td>
                                <td>25%</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </section>

                <section class="content-section">
                    <h2 class="section-title">Beyond the Numbers: Practical Tips for Tracking Macros</h2>
                    <p>Effective macro tracking involves accurately logging your food intake using a food diary or a dedicated nutrition tracking app. Paying attention to serving sizes, using a food scale, and consistently logging meals are key for precision. Focus on whole, unprocessed foods for better nutrient density.</p>
                </section>


                <section class="faq-section content-section">
                    <h2 class="section-title">Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h2>
                    <div class="faq-index card">
                        <h3>FAQ Index</h3>
                        <ul>
                            <li><a href="#faq-item-1">What is Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR)?</a></li>
                            <li><a href="#faq-item-2">What is Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE)?</a></li>
                            <li><a href="#faq-item-3">What is the difference between Mifflin-St Jeor and Harris-Benedict formulas?</a></li>
                            <li><a href="#faq-item-4">How many calories do I need to lose weight?</a></li>
                            <li><a href="#faq-item-5">What are the best macro ratios for building muscle?</a></li>
                            <li><a href="#faq-item-6">How do I track my macros effectively?</a></li>
                            <li><a href="#faq-item-7">Can this calculator help me maintain my weight?</a></li>
                            <li><a href="#faq-item-8">Are these calculations 100% accurate?</a></li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>

                    <div class="faq-item" id="faq-item-1"> 
                        <h3> <button class="accordion" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-panel-1"> What is Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR)? <span class="accordion-icon"></span> </button> </h3>
                        <div class="panel" id="faq-panel-1" role="region"> <p>BMR is the minimum number of calories your body needs to perform basic, life-sustaining functions at rest, such as breathing, circulation, and cell production. It represents the energy expended while in a state of complete rest.</p> </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-item" id="faq-item-2">
                        <h3> <button class="accordion" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-panel-2"> What is Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE)? <span class="accordion-icon"></span> </button> </h3>
                        <div class="panel" id="faq-panel-2" role="region"> <p>TDEE is the total number of calories your body burns in a 24-hour period, including your BMR and the energy expended through physical activity, digestion, and other daily movements. It's the most accurate representation of your daily calorie needs.</p> </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-item" id="faq-item-3">
                        <h3> <button class="accordion" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-panel-3"> What is the difference between Mifflin-St Jeor and Harris-Benedict formulas? <span class="accordion-icon"></span> </button> </h3>
                        <div class="panel" id="faq-panel-3" role="region"> <p>Both are equations used to estimate BMR. The Harris-Benedict equation (revised 1984) was developed earlier and tends to overestimate BMR in some modern populations. The Mifflin-St Jeor equation (1990) is generally considered more accurate for today's average adult, as it was derived from a larger, more diverse dataset. Our calculator allows you to choose either.</p> </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-item" id="faq-item-4">
                        <h3> <button class="accordion" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-panel-4"> How many calories do I need to lose weight? <span class="accordion-icon"></span> </button> </h3>
                        <div class="panel" id="faq-panel-4" role="region"> <p>To lose weight, you generally need to consume fewer calories than your TDEE, creating a calorie deficit. A common guideline is to aim for a 500-750 calorie deficit per day, which typically leads to a weight loss of 1-1.5 pounds per week. Our calculator will provide a target range based on your TDEE and chosen goal.</p> </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-item" id="faq-item-5">
                        <h3> <button class="accordion" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-panel-5"> What are the best macro ratios for building muscle? <span class="accordion-icon"></span> </button> </h3>
                        <div class="panel" id="faq-panel-5" role="region"> <p>For muscle gain, a common strategy involves consuming a calorie surplus (more than your TDEE) combined with a higher protein intake. Typical macro ranges might be 25-35% protein, 40-55% carbohydrates, and 20-30% fat. The exact ratios can vary based on individual response and training intensity.</p> </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-item" id="faq-item-6">
                        <h3> <button class="accordion" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-panel-6"> How do I track my macros effectively? <span class="accordion-icon"></span> </button> </h3>
                        <div class="panel" id="faq-panel-6" role="region"> <p>Effective macro tracking involves accurately logging your food intake using a food diary or a dedicated nutrition tracking app. Paying attention to serving sizes, using a food scale, and consistently logging meals are key for precision. Focus on whole, unprocessed foods for better nutrient density.</p> </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-item" id="faq-item-7">
                        <h3> <button class="accordion" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-panel-7"> Can this calculator help me maintain my weight? <span class="accordion-icon"></span> </button> </h3>
                        <div class="panel" id="faq-panel-7" role="region"> <p>Yes, by selecting the 'Weight Maintenance' goal, the calculator will provide your estimated TDEE. Consuming calories roughly equal to your TDEE is the basis for maintaining your current weight, assuming consistent activity levels.</p> </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-item" id="faq-item-8">
                        <h3> <button class="accordion" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-panel-8"> Are these calculations 100% accurate? <span class="accordion-icon"></span> </button> </h3>
                        <div class="panel" id="faq-panel-8" role="region"> <p>While based on scientifically validated formulas, all BMR and TDEE calculators provide *estimates*. Individual metabolic rates can vary due to genetics, hormonal factors, and body composition (muscle vs. fat). Use the results as a strong starting point and adjust based on your body's response and progress towards your goals.</p> </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

            </article>
        </div> 
